  "decision_conditions": [
    "The development to which this permission relates must be begun not later than the expiration of three years beginning with the date of the decision notice. \rReason: To comply with the provisions of S.51 of the Planning & Compulsory Purchase Act 2004.\r",
    "The development hereby permitted shall be carried out in accordance with the approved plans, as follows:\r\rP0200 A\rP0202 D\rP0204 C\rP0205 C\rP0206 C\rP0207 B\rP0208 B\rP0209 B\rP0210 B\rP0211 B\rTransport Statement, Steer, June 2024\rEdmonton Green Ramp SuDS Strategy Technical Note, Heyne Tillett Steel, 22/10/24\r2788-HTS-00-00-DR-S-4300\r2788-HTS-00-00-DR-S-4301\r2788-HTS-00-00-DR-S-4302\r2788-HTS-00-B2-DR-S-7000\r2788-HTS-00-B2-DR-S-7010\r\rFor information only:\rP0202 C\rP0203 C\rP0204 B\rP0205 B\rP0106 B\rP0207 A\rP0208 A\rP0209 A\rP0210 A\rP0211 A\rP0219 C\rP0220 C\rDesign and Access Statement 1106B AF_DOC 005_REV E 25.06.24\rCulvert Diversion Phasing Plans\r\rReason: For the avoidance of doubt and in the interests of proper planning.\r\r",
    "No development shall be brought into use until a landscaping and public realm scheme has been submitted to and approved in writing by the local planning authority. The scheme shall include, but not be limited to, the following details:\r\ra.\ta statement setting out the design objectives and how these will be delivered; \rb.\thard and soft landscaping;\rc.\tpaving and hard surfacing materials;\rd.\tfurniture, including seating, planters, bollards, refuse units;\re.\tplay areas and play equipment;\rf.\tsigns and wayfinding elements;\rg.\tvehicle, bicycle and pedestrian circulation areas; \rh.\tlighting, floodlighting and CCTV; \ri.\tan implementation and maintenance programme. \r\rThe detailed works shall be carried out in accordance with the approved details within three months of the completion of the herein approved ramp structure. The completed scheme shall be managed and/or maintained in accordance with an approved implementation and maintenance programme.\r\rReason: To ensure that the space beneath the ramp structure presents a suitable standard of amenity and is well integrated into the landscaping and public realm.\r",
    "The development shall not commence until a Sustainable Drainage Strategy has been submitted to and approved in writing by the Local Planning Authority.  The details shall be based on the disposal of surface water by means of a sustainable drainage system in accordance with the principles as set out in the Technical Guidance to the National Planning Policy Framework and should be in line with our DMD Policy SuDS Requirements:\r\ra)\tShall be designed to a 1 in 1 and 1 in 100 year storm event with the allowance for climate change\rb)\tFollow the London Plan Drainage Hierarchy and providing evidence of the potential for full infiltration SuDS and maximising above ground storage\rc)\tFollow the SuDS management train by providing source control for the site, and a number of treatment phases corresponding to their pollution potential \rd)\tMaximise opportunities for sustainable development, improve water quality, biodiversity, local amenity and recreation value\re)\tThe system must be designed to allow for flows that exceed the design capacity to be stored on site or conveyed off-site with minimum impact\rf)\tClear ownership, management and maintenance arrangements must be established\rg)\tThe details submitted shall include levels, sizing, cross sections and specifications for all drainage features\r\rREASON: To ensure the sustainable management of water, minimise flood risk, minimise discharge of surface water outside of the curtilage of the property and ensure that the drainage system will remain functional throughout the lifetime of the development in accordance with Policy CP28 of the Core Strategy, DMD Policy 61, and Policies SI12 & SI13 of the London Plan and the NPPF and to maximise opportunities for sustainable development, improve water quality, biodiversity, local amenity and recreation value\r",
    "The development shall not commence until a Flood Risk Technical Note is submitted to and approved in writing by the Local Planning Authority that includes a detailed Flood Management Plan is in place for the 1 in 100 year plus climate change flood event. This must include an access route in the low hazard area of the floodplain  (as defined by the Environment Agency's Flood Risk Assessment Guidance for New Development R&D Technical Report FD2320) \r\rREASON: To minimise flood risk in accordance with Policy CP28 of the Core Strategy and Policies SI12 of the London Plan, DMD Policy 59, 60 and 62, the SFRA and the NPPF\r",
    "No development shall commence until development within Phase 0 of planning permission ref. 20/04187/OUT, as amended by non-material amendment ref. 25/02309/NMA, has commenced. \r\rReason: To ensure that the herein approved ramp facilitates the development of Edmonton Green Shopping Centre in accordance with planning permission ref. 20/04187/OUT, as amended by non-material amendment ref. 25/02309/NMA.\r",
    "The herein approved ramp shall be permanently removed from the site no later than six months following the completion of construction of the vehicular access bridge to the multi-storey car park on Plot 4 in Phase 3A of planning permission ref. 20/04187/OUT, as amended by non-material amendment ref. 25/02309/NMA. All associated materials and debris shall be cleared from the site.\r\rReason:\rTo prevent the retention of redundant structures, to ensure the site is developed appropriately in line with planning permission ref. 20/04187/OUT, as amended by non-material amendment ref. 25/02309/NMA, and in the interests of user amenity.\r",
    "Prior to the commencement of any construction activities on the site, the developer shall submit a comprehensive Construction Logistics Management Plan (CLMP) to the Planning Authority for approval. The CLMP shall address the following key aspects:\r\r1. Site Access and Egress:\ro Detail the proposed routes for construction vehicles to access and exit the site.\ro Provide measures to minimise traffic congestion and disruptions to the surrounding road network.\ro Specify any proposed temporary road closures or diversions, if applicable.\r\r2. Construction Traffic Management:\ro Outline arrangements for the management of construction traffic, including loading and unloading areas.\ro Confirmation of designated times for deliveries to minimise impact on peak traffic hours.\ro Provide measures to ensure the safe passage of pedestrians and cyclists around the site.\r\r3. Site Layout and Storage:\ro Provide a site layout plan indicating the location of construction activities, storage areas, and welfare facilities.\ro Detail provisions for the storage of materials, equipment, and waste to minimise visual and environmental impact.\ro Specify measures to mitigate noise, dust, and other nuisances generated by construction activities.\r\r4. Construction Schedule:\ro Present a construction schedule outlining the sequence of works and anticipated duration of each phase, including number and type of vehicles required for each stage.\ro Detail the hours of work expected for each phase and the overall duration.\r\r5. Health and Safety:\ro Demonstrate compliance with health and safety regulations, including provisions for worker safety and welfare.\ro Detail measures to mitigate risks associated with construction activities, such as traffic accidents and site hazards.\ro Arrangements for wheel cleaning.\ro Measures to use operators committed to best practice, demonstrated by membership of TfL's Freight Operator Recognition Scheme (FQRS), or similar.\r\rReason: To ensure that the development does not prejudice the safe and efficient operation of the local highway network, does not give rise to unacceptable impacts on highway safety, amenity or the free flow of traffic, and provides appropriate arrangements for the management of construction activities in accordance with Policies T4 and T7 of the London Plan (2021), Policies CP24 and CP25 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), and Policies DMD45 and DMD47 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014)."
  ],
